WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.100
In a market, humans with unlimited wants&nbsp;
often meet sellers with limited supplies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.380
In such a place, competition is usually&nbsp;
a force of good. It leads to more choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.500
increases the quality of products on offer and&nbsp;
reduces their prices. If competition is absent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.200
monopolies can form. And that's a problem.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.880
Adam Smith, the father of modern economics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.280
defined competition as “allocating productive&nbsp;
resources to their most highly valued uses.”

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.700
For sellers, the money they receive when&nbsp;
they sell is more productive than their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.420
product. For buyers, the produce they&nbsp;
get is more productive than their money.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.960
When a deal is done, neither party may&nbsp;
be entirely happy with the agreed price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.120
but at the end both feel better off.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.240
In a competitive market, those who want&nbsp;
something the most, end up getting it.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.140
Competition takes various forms,&nbsp;
as the following story illustrates.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.680
One dealer in a market decides to bring&nbsp;
in bananas to be the first to sell them.&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.780
He is the only one and since people love&nbsp;
bananas, he sells them at a high price.&nbsp;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.620
And it doesn’t take long for someone else&nbsp;
to see an opportunity for improvement.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:21.060
Competing on price is the most straightforward&nbsp;
way in which sellers can compete for customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.700
because consumers usually prefer to pay less.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.380
In order to survive, the first&nbsp;
seller, also known as “incumbent”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.900
may also lower the prices. This pressure on&nbsp;
prices is usually leaving customers better off.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.900
In theory, this back and forth will continue until&nbsp;
both have reached a price that yields zero profit.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.120
Then one may decide to close.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.920
But there is also Non-price competition.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.860
Instead of offering a lower price, newcomers can&nbsp;
try to sell higher quality products or innovate.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.040
In response to innovators, incumbents may try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.040
improve their offers and come up with&nbsp;
ideas to strengthen their position.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.620
Both may increase their service hours.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.360
So besides lowering prices, another&nbsp;
consequence of competition is the range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.960
of choices and innovation; both of which&nbsp;
are good for the people in the market.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.100
In a free market, competition defines the&nbsp;
ultimate price and quality of goods on offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.860
a phenomenon referred to as emergent order.&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.420
That means, it’s eventually not up to the shop&nbsp;
to decide how much to charge for a product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.820
but intangible forces such as supply, demand&nbsp;
and consumer preferences that emerge over time.&nbsp;

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:34.620
If one company is the only one selling&nbsp;
a product, we speak of a monopoly.&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.240
Then the benefits of competition fade, and&nbsp;
the naturally emergent order gets disrupted.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.640
From perfect competition to&nbsp;
monopoly lies a spectrum we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.040
can use to define the intensity of competition

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.060
In a highly transparent market we&nbsp;
have almost perfect competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.400
with low prices and lots of consumer choice.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.180
In a market that is dominated by&nbsp;
a monopoly, prices are often high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.500
and the variety of options decreases.
If just a few companies share the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.540
we speak of Oligopoly, which is also a&nbsp;
problem as they often secretly fix prices.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.160
In a free and open market with high competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.320
we see innovative suppliers, happy customers&nbsp;
and an immersive variety of choice.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.240
On the other end of the&nbsp;
spectrum, we have a monopoly.&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.740
Suppliers have little bargaining power,&nbsp;
the goods they sell are often inferior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.680
customers pay higher prices and wait longer hours.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.480
In order for markets to function, governments need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.260
to break monopolies up and consumers&nbsp;
should support those that rival them.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.740
Only then will we see innovation, good products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:45.240
low prices, happy customers and&nbsp;
markets that leave everyone better off.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:53.100
In summary: Competition usually leads to lower&nbsp;
prices, innovation and higher living standards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.900
Monopolies usually lead to higher prices,&nbsp;
inferior products and lower living standards&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.680
We should try to break up monopolies&nbsp;
to support free market competition.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.400
To learn more about competition,&nbsp;
tell us how you would compete!

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.000
First find a product or service available&nbsp;
online or in your neighborhood with very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.480
little competition. Describe the&nbsp;
service and tell us why you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.680
there is little competition. Is it because of&nbsp;
a monopoly? Or because the demand is so low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.980
that there is little profit to be&nbsp;
made? Or is there another reason?

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.760
Now assume you are opening&nbsp;
a competing business. Tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.340
us 3 things that you would do in order to compete.

